WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.663
Start with delulu, job, interview,

00:00:03.683 --> 00:00:06.936
expectations. Someone adds foolish as a

00:00:06.956 --> 00:00:09.410
supposed alternative for delulu.

00:00:09.598 --> 00:00:11.575
Does adding this word make the search

00:00:11.595 --> 00:00:14.217
safer because the original terms are still

00:00:14.237 --> 00:00:16.988
there? Try to predict what happens under

00:00:17.008 --> 00:00:18.933
two different admission rules.

00:00:21.341 --> 00:00:23.305
The same weak term can cause two different

00:00:23.325 --> 00:00:24.490
kinds of damage.

00:00:24.639 --> 00:00:27.196
A compulsory term can exclude a record.

00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:29.516
An optional scoring term can shift the

00:00:29.536 --> 00:00:32.313
competition. Neither result requires the

00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:34.090
engine to malfunction.

00:00:35.008 --> 00:00:37.370
Meet two records from the chapter's BM

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.698
twenty-five Evidence Lab.

00:00:38.718 --> 00:00:42.164
Record A is Delulu expectations during a

00:00:42.184 --> 00:00:44.639
job interview. It contains all four

00:00:44.659 --> 00:00:47.525
original query terms and no foolish.

00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:50.592
Record F is Why foolish job applicants

00:00:50.612 --> 00:00:51.996
misread the interview.

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:56.004
F contains foolish, job and interview, but

00:00:56.024 --> 00:00:58.890
lacks delulu and expectations.

00:00:59.052 --> 00:01:00.669
These are teaching records from a

00:01:00.689 --> 00:01:02.124
six-record collection.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.058
Their usefulness still depends on the

00:01:04.078 --> 00:01:07.138
searcher's need; term matches alone do not

00:01:07.158 --> 00:01:08.516
settle that judgment.

00:01:09.413 --> 00:01:11.984
Under implied AND, all five terms are

00:01:12.004 --> 00:01:14.742
compulsory. A passed the original four

00:01:14.762 --> 00:01:18.066
conditions. Adding foolish now excludes it

00:01:18.086 --> 00:01:19.870
before ranking begins.

00:01:20.032 --> 00:01:23.144
F fails too, because it lacks delulu and

00:01:23.164 --> 00:01:24.432
expectations.

00:01:24.973 --> 00:01:27.172
Imagine A is exactly what you needed.

00:01:27.321 --> 00:01:29.492
A better ranker cannot recover it from

00:01:29.512 --> 00:01:32.262
this candidate set: A never arrived.

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.925
You would need to change the retrieval

00:01:33.945 --> 00:01:37.185
conditions or run another retrieval pass.

00:01:38.102 --> 00:01:40.737
Switch to any-match BM twenty-five.

00:01:40.886 --> 00:01:43.276
A remains eligible through its original

00:01:43.296 --> 00:01:46.639
matches. F also remains eligible through

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:50.041
its matches. Adding foolish gives F an

00:01:50.061 --> 00:01:52.361
additional matching term that contributes

00:01:52.381 --> 00:01:53.391
to its score.

00:01:53.935 --> 00:01:55.790
A has not been excluded, but its

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.748
competition can change.

00:01:57.911 --> 00:02:00.391
We are not asserting a numerical order for

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:03.831
A and F here. Admission and scoring

00:02:03.851 --> 00:02:06.074
influence are enough to explain the two

00:02:06.094 --> 00:02:07.297
different risks.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:11.135
In the lab's six-record collection, only F

00:02:11.155 --> 00:02:12.707
contains foolish.

00:02:12.856 --> 00:02:15.388
A rare term can receive high inverse

00:02:15.408 --> 00:02:18.030
document frequency, giving its matches

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.594
extra weight. But rarity answers a

00:02:20.614 --> 00:02:23.249
collection question: how widely does this

00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:24.150
term occur?

00:02:24.698 --> 00:02:25.913
It does not answer the searcher's

00:02:25.933 --> 00:02:28.284
question: is this a good way to express my

00:02:28.304 --> 00:02:31.003
need? A statistical weight cannot turn a

00:02:31.023 --> 00:02:33.593
weak substitute into a good concept.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.428
More weight can amplify a poor choice.

00:02:37.341 --> 00:02:40.177
Could term-frequency saturation fix it?

00:02:40.352 --> 00:02:43.012
Saturation limits the extra benefit of

00:02:43.032 --> 00:02:46.452
repeating the same term inside a document.

00:02:46.628 --> 00:02:49.339
That is useful when twenty mentions should

00:02:49.359 --> 00:02:51.542
not get twenty times the credit.

00:02:51.692 --> 00:02:52.921
It is a different problem.

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:56.368
Saturation does not inspect whether

00:02:56.388 --> 00:02:58.970
foolish represents the intended concept.

00:02:59.133 --> 00:03:01.664
A weak query term can still contribute

00:03:01.684 --> 00:03:02.952
lexical evidence.

00:03:03.076 --> 00:03:05.942
Neither a smooth score nor a sophisticated

00:03:05.962 --> 00:03:08.713
ranking formula guarantees a well-formed

00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:09.486
query.

00:03:10.397 --> 00:03:12.321
Compare the search with and without the

00:03:12.341 --> 00:03:15.375
weak term. Inspect which records enter,

00:03:15.576 --> 00:03:18.275
which disappear, and which gain scoring

00:03:18.295 --> 00:03:20.968
evidence. Keep the collection and other

00:03:20.988 --> 00:03:23.378
settings fixed so the comparison has a

00:03:23.398 --> 00:03:24.717
clear interpretation.

00:03:25.283 --> 00:03:27.367
Then ask what job the extra term is doing.

00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:29.894
Is it compulsory, one alternative in a

00:03:29.914 --> 00:03:32.831
group, or optional scoring evidence?

00:03:32.994 --> 00:03:35.126
The engine can execute your instructions

00:03:35.146 --> 00:03:38.218
correctly while those instructions express

00:03:38.238 --> 00:03:39.339
the wrong need.

00:03:39.501 --> 00:03:42.097
Inspect the proposed expansion before

00:03:42.117 --> 00:03:43.089
trusting it.
